# `module::Model::visit`

[Back to `module::Model`](../Model.md)

Applies a function to whichever struct the model holds.

## Signature

```cpp
template <typename F> decltype(auto) module::Model::visit(F &&f);
template <typename F> decltype(auto) module::Model::visit(F &&f) const;
```

## What It Does

Calls `f` with the held struct (`model::Heston &`, `model::Sabr &`, ...), so one generic lambda handles every family (typically with `if constexpr` on the type). It is `std::visit` on the underlying variant. The non-const form lets `f` edit the struct.

## Parameters

`F &&f`
: A callable accepting each of the six structs.

## Returns

Whatever `f` returns (the same type for every family).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Classifying whatever the model holds. One generic lambda returns a label for every family.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>
#include <type_traits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  for (const auto &m : {fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}), fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Bates, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04, 0.5, -0.1, 0.2})}) {
    const std::string label = m.visit([](const auto &s) -> std::string {
      if constexpr (std::is_same_v<std::decay_t<decltype(s)>, fin::model::BlackScholes>) return "constant volatility";
      else return "stochastic volatility";
    });
    out.writeln(m.name(), ":", label);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

Type-dependent behaviour with `if constexpr`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <type_traits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const mod::Model m = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04});
  m.visit([&](const auto &s) {
    using T = std::decay_t<decltype(s)>;
    if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, fin::model::Heston>) out.writeln("long-run volatility =", std::sqrt(s.theta.template items<double>()[0]));
    else out.writeln("not Heston");
  });
}
```

### Example 3

Editing through the non-const form: scale every Heston volatility-of-variance by 2.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <type_traits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(3));
  mod::Model m = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04});
  m.visit([](auto &s) {
    if constexpr (std::is_same_v<std::decay_t<decltype(s)>, fin::model::Heston>) s.sigma = s.sigma * 2.0;
  });
  out.writeln(m.parameters().items<double>());
}
```

